        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d6f9db80-d82d-57be-a00d-1369d4f880eb",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: High Tider, American English dialects\n- Places: South Atlantic United States, North Carolina Down East, Outer Banks, Pamlico Sound, Carteret County, Atlantic, Sea Level, Harkers Island, Ocracoke, Chesapeake Bay, Tangier, Smith Island\n\nFacts:\n- High Tider, also called \"Hoi Toider,\" is a dialect of American English.\n- High Tider is spoken in very limited communities in the South Atlantic United States.\n- High Tider is spoken in small island and coastal townships of North Carolina's Down East region, including Atlantic, Sea Level, Harkers Island, and Ocracoke.\n- North Carolina's Down East region encompasses the Outer Banks and Pamlico Sound.\n- High Tider is also spoken in Chesapeake Bay communities such as Tangier and Smith Island.\n- Since 1850, Tangier Island in Virginia has lost 67% of its landmass.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
